# GI01A — Explain v3 ("Learn" depth): acute liver failure, part 1 — the definition, the timing systems (Trey & Davidson, Bernuau, O’Grady, Japanese), the O’Grady paradox, IASL and
# Indian definitions, causes (five groups), commonest causes East and West, the AIIMS series, drugs/toxins/herbs, cause-specific treatment, presentation, organ effects, work-up, true/false sweep.
# No mnemonics or hooks. Driven by qpack/GI01A.md (19 question links; ALF-10 cause-specific treatment asked 4 times, ALF-06 causes 3; ALF-01, -02, -03, -09 and -13 twice each; ALF-07 and
# ALF-08 once). ALF-04, -05, -11, -12 and -14 have no linked question and get short explanations with [pattern] decode items.
# No `fix` entries for GI01A in trace/test/data/qbank.json (checked 20 Nov 2026); the gi_cardlayer.json `lines` for ALF-06, -09, -10 and -13 were read and are covered and agreed with.
# No bank Top-up cards exist for GI01A in gi_topups.json (checked 20 Nov 2026), so there is no topups file.
# Checked online on 20 Nov 2026:
#  - AASLD position paper update 2011 (Lee, Larson, Stravitz; AASLD PDF read): INR ≥1.5 + any encephalopathy, no pre-existing cirrhosis, illness <26 weeks; Wilson, vertically acquired HBV and
#    AIH count despite possible cirrhosis if recognised <26 weeks; charcoal 1 g/kg (best within 1 h, useful to 3–4 h; recommendation: within 4 h, just before NAC); aminotransferases >3,500 IU/L
#    highly correlated with paracetamol; Amanita: penicillin G 300,000–1 million units/kg/day IV, silibinin 30–40 mg/kg/day for 3–4 days; HSV: aciclovir 5–10 mg/kg IV 8-hourly; HBV:
#    nucleos(t)ide analogues ‘may be considered’, prophylaxis for HBsAg-positive patients starting chemotherapy/immunosuppression; AIH: prednisone 40–60 mg/day, must not delay transplant
#    evaluation, biopsy if autoantibodies negative; AFLP/HELLP: expeditious delivery; Wilson: bilirubin (mg/dL) : ALP (IU/L) >2, very low ALP or uric acid, Coombs-negative haemolysis,
#    Kayser–Fleischer rings in about 50%, penicillamine not recommended; Budd–Chiari with fulminant failure = transplant indication ‘provided malignancy is excluded’; transjugular biopsy for
#    suspected AIH, metastatic disease, lymphoma, HSV, indeterminate cause; Table 2 initial tests; circulation: low SVR.
#  - EASL 2017 ALF guideline (J Hepatol; full text via a university mirror; easl.eu itself blocked): INR usually >1.5 with HE in a liver without chronic disease; jaundice-to-HE hyperacute
#    ≤7 days, acute 8–28 days, subacute 5–12 weeks, beyond 28 weeks = chronic; hyperacute most severe coagulopathy and best spontaneous recovery, subacute deepest jaundice, worst recovery,
#    lowest ICH risk; causes by phenotype (paracetamol, HAV, HEV hyperacute; HBV acute; non-paracetamol DILI subacute); ICH risks (hyperacute/acute, young, renal failure, inotropes, persistent
#    high arterial ammonia); HBV the commonest viral cause; AIH: steroids, list if no improvement within 7 days; AFLP/HELLP: prompt delivery; Wilson: Coombs-negative haemolysis, high
#    bilirubin:ALP ratio, low uric acid; Amanita and phosphorus among toxins; biopsy limited, transjugular.
#  - ACG guideline (Shingina et al., Am J Gastroenterol 2023; Healio summary): no pre-existing liver disease; NAC for paracetamol and suggested for non-paracetamol ALF; antivirals for HBV reactivation;
#    no routine coagulopathy correction; King’s or MELD >25 for prognosis; early transfer. No newer ACG or AASLD ALF guideline found for 2025–26.
#  - Japanese criteria (Mochida/Sugawara, J Gastroenterol 2012; PMC full text): counted from the ONSET OF SYMPTOMS: fulminant hepatitis = grade II+ HE within 8 weeks with PT ≤40%; acute type
#    within 10 days, subacute 11–56 days; late-onset hepatic failure 8–24 weeks; 2011 ALF criteria PT ≤40% or INR ≥1.5.
#  - IASL 1999 (Tandon; as quoted in a PMC review): hyperacute <10 days, fulminant 10–30 days, subacute hepatic failure 5–24 weeks (card: more than 4 to 24 weeks).
#  - Bhatia, Singhal, Panda, Acharya, Hepatology 2008;48:1577 (IAS repository abstract): 1,015 ALF patients at AIIMS 1986–2006, 249 pregnant; HEV 59.4% in pregnant women vs 30.4% non-pregnant
#    women and 23.1% men; mortality 53.8% vs 57.2% vs 57.9% (NS); HEV-ALF in pregnancy 51%; trimester no effect. (PubMed page for the same paper was blocked.)
#  - Overview of ALF in India (2021, PMC): INASL definition = encephalopathy within 4 weeks of symptom onset, no prior liver disease; AIIMS 1986–2015 (n 1,462): HEV 28.7%, indeterminate 36%,
#    HBV 8.8%, ATT 7.0%, HAV 2%; INDILI DILI-ALF (n 124): anti-TB drugs 63%, CAM 12.1%.
# From standard references without a fresh check (confident unless said): Trey & Davidson 1970 (8 weeks from symptoms), Bernuau 1986 (<2 weeks, 2–12 weeks from jaundice), O’Grady 1993 (Lancet);
# ALFSG paracetamol 39% (Ostapowicz 2002), about 46% in later ALFSG reports; amatoxin RNA polymerase II inhibition and OATP1B3 uptake; halothane trifluoroacetyl adducts; factor VII half-life
# about 6 hours, factor V not vitamin K-dependent; ammonia–glutamine astrocyte swelling, arterial ammonia >200 µmol/L and herniation (Clemmesen 1999); relative adrenal insufficiency in ALF
# (Harry 2002); Korman 2008 Wilson ratios (ALP:bilirubin <4, AST:ALT >2.2); anti-LKM1 in type 2 AIH; HSV hepatitis often anicteric. The card’s AIIMS percentages table (ALF-08) and the UK 57%
# figure (ALF-07) were not traced to a primary source.
# Independent review, 20 Nov 2026 (second checker): Japanese criteria re-read (J Gastroenterol 2012, Springer full text: intervals from the onset of disease symptoms; acute type ≤10 days,
#    subacute 11–56 days, LOHF 8–24 weeks; PT ≤40% or INR ≥1.5); EASL 2017 PDF (easl.eu): O’Grady intervals 7 days / 8–28 days / 5–12 weeks, >28 weeks chronic, ICH risk with persistent arterial
#    ammonia >200 µmol/L, ICP monitoring if 150–200 not falling, neuro checks and glucose ~2-hourly, hourly urine, HBV commonest viral cause (European setting), AIH list if no improvement in 7 days;
#    AASLD 2011 PDF: bilirubin:ALP >2, hourly neuro checks without ICP monitoring, aminotransferases and bilirubin daily, ammonia >200 linked with herniation (Clemmesen); Clemmesen 1999 abstract
#    (herniation 230 ± 58 vs 118 ± 48 µmol/L); Korman, Hepatology 2008 abstract (ALP:bilirubin <4: 94%/96%; AST:ALT >2.2: 94%/86%; combined 100%); Patterson, BMJ Open 2020 (viral ALF: HEV 32%,
#    HAV 21%, HBV 20%); Acharya, J Gastroenterol Hepatol 2002 abstract (paracetamol ~70% UK, ~20% USA); UK transplant-unit series (PMC2742613: paracetamol 61.2%, USA 39%).

SPINE = [
  ("What counts as acute liver failure", "Coagulopathy with any encephalopathy, in a liver that was normal, within 26 weeks, is acute liver failure; without encephalopathy it is only acute liver injury."),
  ("The clock sets the phenotype", "Each system times the illness from a different event, and the speed of onset predicts both the danger to the brain and the chance of recovering without a transplant."),
  ("Causes differ East and West", "Paracetamol dominates in the West, hepatitis E and anti-tubercular drugs in India, and a large share of Indian cases has no cause found."),
  ("Some causes have specific treatments", "Paracetamol, hepatitis B, herpes viruses, Amanita, autoimmune hepatitis, pregnancy and Wilson disease each change the first order written, and some of them mean listing at once."),
  ("How the failing liver presents", "Encephalopathy without stigmata of chronic disease, with hypoglycaemia, a hyperdynamic circulation, renal failure and immunoparesis, is the picture of a whole-body illness."),
  ("Work-up, monitoring and the sweep", "The first tests measure severity (INR, factor V, ammonia, lactate), hunt for a treatable cause, and are repeated often because the course can turn within hours."),
]

PRIMER = "The liver makes almost all clotting factors (factor VII has the shortest half-life, about 6 hours, so the INR rises within hours of losing hepatocyte function), clears ammonia and lactate, and makes glucose between meals. When most hepatocytes die at once in a liver that was previously normal, all of these fail together: the INR climbs, ammonia reaches the brain and swells the astrocytes, and glucose falls. Survival depends on whether the remaining liver regenerates before the brain swells or sepsis sets in, which is why the speed of onset and the cause matter so much. This deck covers what counts as acute liver failure, how fast it came, what caused it and the first tests; prognostic criteria, ICU care and transplant follow in GI01B–C."

